#=GF DR   SO; 0000603; group_II_intron;
#=GF DR   GO; 0000373; Group II intron splicing;
#=GF RN   [1]
#=GF RM   11377794
#=GF RT   The ins and outs of group II introns.
#=GF RA   Bonen L, Vogel J
#=GF RL   Trends Genet 2001;17:322-331.
#=GF RN   [2]
#=GF RM   11726522
#=GF RT   Control of branch-site choice by a group II intron.
#=GF RA   Chu VT, Adamidi C, Liu Q, Perlman PS, Pyle AM
#=GF RL   EMBO J 2001;20:6866-6876.
#=GF RN   [3]
#=GF RM   12870716
#=GF RT   Group II introns: structure and catalytic versatility of large natural
#=GF RT   ribozymes.
#=GF RA   Lehmann K, Schmidt U
#=GF RL   Crit Rev Biochem Mol Biol 2003;38:249-303.
#=GF CC   Group II introns are found in rRNA, tRNA and mRNA of organelles in fungi,
#=GF CC   plants and protists, and also in mRNA in bacteria. They are large
#=GF CC   self-splicing ribozymes and have 6 structural domains (usually designated
#=GF CC   dI to dVI. This model and alignment represents only domains V and VI. A
#=GF CC   subset of group II introns also encode essential splicing proteins in
#=GF CC   intronic ORFs. The length of these introns can therefore be up to 3kb.
#=GF CC   Splicing occurs in almost identical fashion to nuclear pre-mRNA splicing
#=GF CC   with two transesterification steps. The 2' hydroxyl of a bulged adenosine
#=GF CC   in domain VI attacks the 5' splice site, followed by nucleophilic attack
#=GF CC   on the 3' splice site by the 3' OH of the upstream exon. Protein machinery
#=GF CC   is required for splicing in vivo, and long range intron-intron and
#=GF CC   intron-exon interactions are important for splice site positioning. Group
#=GF CC   II introns are further sub-classified into groups IIA and IIB which differ
#=GF CC   in splice site consensus, distance of bulged A from 3' splice site, some
#=GF CC   tertiary interactions, and intronic ORF phylogeny [1].
#=GF WK   Group_II_intron
#=GF SQ   406

